{% extends "layouts/catalog.html.jinja" %}
{% from "includes/page-header.html.jinja" import render_page_header with context %}
{% from "includes/block-example.html.jinja" import render_block_example %}
{% from "includes/code-snippet.html.jinja" import render_code_snippet %}
{% from "pages/blocks/previews/overlay-scrollbar.html.jinja" import overlay_scrollbar_demo with context %}

{% block title %}Overlay Scrollbar — Moo UI{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
  {{ render_page_header("Overlay Scrollbar", "A quiet scrollbar without a layout gutter. The document or native scroll region still owns scrolling.") }}

  <div class="moo-component-examples">
    {% set demo %}{{ overlay_scrollbar_demo() }}{% endset %}
    {% set public_source %}{{ overlay_scrollbar_demo(controls=false) }}{% endset %}
    {{ render_block_example(
      "overlay-scrollbar-document",
      "Document scrolling",
      "Scroll inside the preview, or use Open standalone to try the same example as a full page. Its theme, direction and native-scrollbar controls stay inside the example.",
      demo,
      preview_class="moo-example__preview--shell",
      frame_src=site_href("blocks/previews/overlay-scrollbar.html", root_path),
      frame_width=1280,
      frame_height=900,
      source_content=public_source
    ) }}

    <h2 id="public-module" class="h4 mt-5">Opt-in public module</h2>
    {% set initialization %}
import OverlayScrollbar from "@wpmoo/ui/overlay-scrollbar.js";

const owner = document.querySelector(".moo-ui");
const scrollbar = OverlayScrollbar.getOrCreateInstance(owner);

// scrollbar.refresh(); // after host geometry changes
// scrollbar.dispose(); // restore the native scrollbar
    {% endset %}
    {{ render_code_snippet("overlay-scrollbar-initialization", initialization, language="js") }}
    <p>Place the example markup inside your existing <code>.moo-ui</code> theme owner and load the stylesheet before initialization. There is one instance per native scroll target, attached to one explicit theme owner. The default is the standards-mode document; pass <code>scrollElement</code> to use an existing native scrolling region, like the catalog's Page main. No replacement viewport is created. Showing and hiding the thumb never changes content width; enabling it initially removes the native gutter.</p>
    <p>Touch and wheel scrolling stay native. Reduced-motion preferences remove the fade transition. Forced-colors mode, printing and horizontal page overflow keep the native scrollbar instead. Disabling the enhancement restores it.</p>
    <h2 id="options" class="h4 mt-4">Options and theme tokens</h2>
    <p><code>hideDelay</code> defaults to 900 milliseconds; <code>label</code> supplies the localized accessible name. The default is “Page scroll position”. Importing the module does not initialize anything.</p>
    <p>Bootstrap does not provide an overlay scrollbar. Its two geometry tokens are <code>--moo-scrollbar-overlay-thumb-size</code> (backed by <code>$moo-scrollbar-overlay-thumb-size</code>, inheriting the shared thin-scrollbar scale) and <code>--moo-scrollbar-overlay-hit-size</code> (backed by <code>$moo-scrollbar-overlay-hit-size</code>, a 24px interaction target). Color uses Bootstrap's secondary/emphasis text tokens, radius uses its pill token, edge spacing uses Bootstrap's smallest positive spacing step, and motion uses its reduced-motion-aware fade transition.</p>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
